Layer 5: scenario runner

The runner is the loop that holds everything together. The shared
loop lives in run/run.h. Each check/ scenario carries its own
frozen copy of the same loop (26 files carry the hold() helper as
byte-identical copies; the rest use the same arms-on-first-call
pattern inline). New YAML scenarios configure
the shared loop instead of copying it.

The loop, in plain words

Each check/ scenario builds a poll vector with two halves: the first
half watches the TUN file descriptors, the second half watches the
child log pipes. The shared runner holds the same vector in Run.p
(run/run.h:15-25). Both repeat until verdict or deadline:

1. Wait up to 100 ms for anything to happen.
2. For each ready TUN, pump one packet (or drop it, if the fault
   schedule says so).
3. For each ready log pipe, read a chunk and scan for markers.
4. Check the phase machine: does the current phase's exit condition
   hold (meshed, settled, submitted, converged)? If yes, advance.
5. Check the children are still alive. If none are, stop.
6. Check the deadline. If passed, stop and fail.

Queries against the system (workload lists, node lists, journal
greps) are throttled to about one round every 2 s through
run_throttle (run/run.h:100). They shell out, so running them
every 100 ms drowns the machine they measure.

The shared entry points are run_setup_isolated (run/run.h:81-83)
for setup, run_poll (run/run.h:89-90) for one pump plus log step,
run_hold (run/run.h:95) for waits, run_alive (run/run.h:107)
for liveness, and run_teardown (run/run.h:137-138) for teardown.
Setup reports RUN_OK, RUN_SKIP, or RUN_FAIL (run/run.h:30-34).
Failure forensics run through RunForensics (run/run.h:46) before
the statedir wipe.

Phases and holds

A phase machine is just an integer plus exit conditions. In check/,
phase 0 is nearly always "wait for mesh plus settle": the seen3
flag (full mesh observed) gates hold(), which arms on first call
and fires HOLD_UP seconds later. Later phases follow the property:
submit, verify spread, land the fault, verify the wound, heal, verify
reunion. On every transition the scenario resets its timers and
observation flags so stale state never leaks across phases.

In YAML, a phase is a mapping with name, do, until or
sustain, hold_s, and timeout_s (suite/exec.c:1475-1624). One
condition per phase in v1; chain phases for AND (suite/exec.h:6-13).
until waits for the condition, then waits out the full hold_s.
hold_s needs until and must not exceed timeout_s. sustain
holds the whole timeout_s: any violation fails at once. One
submit per file in v1. Unknown needs means skip.

Teardown order

The order is invariant across check/ scenarios and the shared runner,
and it must stay that way:

1. SIGTERM each child, reap it, close its TUN and log descriptors.
   (Dead-by-fault pids are skipped or already reaped; each scenario
   knows its own dead.)
2. Free the simulation context.
3. Delete the network namespaces.
4. Wipe the state directory.
5. Report the verdict.

Failure runs forensics between steps 1 and 4: error lines from logs,
peer-count histograms, journal dumps, or per-round journal
preservation (check/concord_spam.c copies journals aside before
wiping). The shared runner calls the RunForensics callback there
instead. Pass runs wipe silently. This ordering matters: forensics
needs the files, and the files die at step 4.

Skips are not failures

A check/ scenario returns success without testing anything when its
preconditions are absent: no ./bin/concord, no TUN permission, no
test image, tiny host memory. CI reads "skip" as "not applicable
here", which keeps the same suite green on laptops and harness
hosts. The shared runner keeps three distinct exits: pass, fail, and
skip with a reason (RUN_OK, RUN_FAIL, RUN_SKIP in
run/run.h:30-34, and SUITE_PASS, SUITE_FAIL, SUITE_SKIP in
suite/exec.h:53-59).

What a YAML scenario configures

Looking across the 33 check/ Concord scenarios, a scenario configures
exactly these things and nothing else:

- Node count and which spawn variant to use.
- The fault schedule: which descriptors drop when, which nodes move
  where, who gets killed and restarted how.
- The observation set: which markers, which journal queries, which
  CLI polls.
- The phase machine: ordered phases with exit conditions and holds.
- The verdict: which flags must hold at the end, and what forensics
  to run if they do not.

Everything else (poll vector, pump calls, throttling, liveness,
teardown, skips) is identical and lives in run/run.h. The proof is
scenarios/*.yaml plus suite/examples/*.yaml, run with
bin/rsuite run. There are no scenarios/*.c files and no
bin/suite_* targets; YAML plus bin/rsuite replaced them.
bin/rsuite lint validates without privileges, bin/rsuite list
prints a parse-only summary, and bin/rsuite doctor reports on the
setup. Porting one simple check/ scenario (two or partition)
against the shared runner is the proof that the extraction is
faithful: same verdicts, no copied loop. suite/examples/concord_two.yaml
is that port for the two-node mesh.
